A||N||15||FieroSTETZ||can you bump start a fiero?||||1||
Z||000000||FieroSTETZ||04-18-2001||10:33 AM||||i was just reading another post here on the forum, and there was talk of push starting a fiero - i know you would have to bypass the clutch thingy, but isn't the fuel pump electric? if the battery is dead, how do you get fuel?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Ken Wittlief||04-18-2001||10:45 AM||||you would have to push it fast enough for the altenator to put out enough power to run the electronics.<P>But that is not ususally a problem. If the battery has enough juice left to light the dome lights or the parking lights it will run the engine electronics, it just wont have 300 amps to drive the starter. Push starting is also a life saver if your starter or solonoide is shot.<P>The clutch bypass?! you put the car in neutral, turn the key to run, get the car moving a few mph (downhill helps), jump in, push the clutch in, drop it in SECOND gear, and pop the clutch. If you try it in first gear the compresssion of the engine will usually skid the car to a stop, instead of spinning the engine over.<P>BTW, cant do this at all with an automatic, another good reason to drive a manual tranny car! (a manny-tranny?)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ken Wittlief||04-18-2001||10:53 AM||||the clutch switch only prevents you from cranking the starter when the clutch is not pressed. When you push start a car you dont use the starter (and you dont turn the key all the way, you only turn it to the run postition).||||reg||1
Z||000003||84Bill||04-18-2001||10:57 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>you would have to push it fast enough for the altenator to put out enough power to run the electronics.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Only if there is enough juice to power the field. if the battery is completly dead poping it at 100 mph won't start the car.<P>you don,t need to bypass anything to popstart a stick fiero. I parked my 86 on downgrades for almost a month and never had  to buypass anything. There was an occasion where I ran out of gas about 300 feet from a gas station and I had to pull the clutch switch out of it's mount hole to crank the engine while in first gear but it got me there that prolly explains why my starter went bad... Go figure.||||reg||6
Z||000004||Ken Wittlief||04-18-2001||11:15 AM||||for a month?! Okay you got me beat. I had a 66 VW microbus with a dead starter, and drove it for 2 weeks the same way (got paid once a month in the Coast Guard), parking on enough of a hill and making sure I couldnt get parked in.<P>  Its a little un-nerving at red lights though, knowing if you stall it your gonna have to get out and push.<P>BTW, 30 seconds in neutral is about the limit for any starter. It would have been better when you 'cranked' your car to the gas pump if you had cranked it for about 20 seconds, then let it sit a few minutes to cool down, another 20 seconds...<P>that is also the method that is used if your clutch link/hydralics gives out, but then you can push the pedal to the floor and crank it while its in gear.<P>the real fun is shifing without the clutch. If you get the rpms just right the tranny drops right into gear with no effort. If you get the rpms wrong the tranny just drops.||||reg||1
Z||000005||walsh2001||04-18-2001||02:02 PM||||I have done this- <P>Bump starting the Fiero is not a problem. I had a guy with a truck match bumbers with me, turned the key to on, put it in 3rd. I depressed the clutch pedal, and when he had pushed me up to 30 I dropped it. Car started right away. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ken Wittlief||04-18-2001||02:47 PM||||circle back, pick up whats left of your bumper and tie it to the roof, and be on your way.<P>30mph! Yikes!  5 to 6 mph is plenty fast in second gear.||||reg||11
Z||000007||johnt671||04-18-2001||03:02 PM||||I've also pushed the car and got it to start.  In one case when my clutch went bad I put the car in 3rd and cranked the starter to move the car.  To my surprise the car started and I drove it 15 miles in 3rd.  Lucky the was only two yields, one right turn and one left turn with no stop signs. <P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||84Bill||04-18-2001||03:24 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B><BR>BTW, 30 seconds in neutral is about the limit for any starter. It would have been better when you 'cranked' your car to the gas pump if you had cranked it for about 20 seconds, then let it sit a few minutes to cool down, another 20 seconds...<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Heh try like a minute and a half at a time. That starter got just a tad on the warm side I let her cool for a few minutes and went at it again, the *****  of it all was the incline at the gas stations driveway kinda short but steep and the battery was hurting pretty bad by then. pusherd it to the pump, gased her up and hit the highway<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||phreakboy4||04-18-2001||04:09 PM||||It actually is possible to bump start a automatic but you have to manage to get the car between 30-40 mph<P><BR>84 2M4 coupe                  Sean V||||reg||1
Z||000010||pHoOl||04-18-2001||05:01 PM||||I've done this a couple times (not in a fiero) and i always drop it into first gear.  Is this second gear thing true for all cars?  i didn't think you'd be rolling enough to turn it over.  Maybe i'll steal my roommates car and give it a try.||||reg||1
Z||000011||fyerstarter||04-18-2001||09:54 PM||||Its easier in second. Keep in mind also you can bump it in reverse if you get in a tricky situation  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||Spektyr||04-18-2001||10:22 PM||||My experience push starting cars:<P>1st gear: Very tricky, you have to pop the clutch, then stomp it back in before the engine stalls itself (the gear is low enough that the sudden pull of the tranny on the motor can make it stop).<P>2nd gear: Most reliable gear for push starting.  In my Nissan PU if I popped the clutch and stomped the gas down, the truck just lit the back end and took off like a shot.  (But then it normally lit the back end in 2nd, and usually 3rd.)<P>Reverse: A fun little trick out of steep driveways.  I always just made the excuse I didn't want to overwork the starter.  Wouldn't recommend trying to start your car like this unless it's already on an incline.  The mechanics of trying to push a car backwards and then hop into it... not smart.<P>Automatics:  You can roll start an automatic provided you can meet 2 criteria.  First off, you're traveling at 30-40 mph.  Second, the engine was running about 10 seconds ago.  If you don't believe me, try this:  Drive along on an open stretch of road (don't try this on a busy street).  Get up to 40-50 mph and turn the key off.  Turn it right back on, bingo the car is running.  Now do it again, but count to 15.  Oops... gotta start it up with the starter.<P>If your torque converter ain't turning, you won't start the car, and it will stop really quickly.  Remember, if you shut the motor off in drive, the car still rolls.  Try doing that in a stick in gear with your foot off the clutch (if you're going to replace the tranny anyway).  That's the big difference.  The torque converter DOESN'T have to turn as fast as the tranny.  It can stop while you're in drive and the car is rolling.<P>(on the outside chance 'torque converter' is the wrong name for the part I'm thinking of, it's the big impeller-like part on the front of the tranny that translates the power from the flywheel to the transmission smoothly)||||reg||1
Z||000013||pontiacs4me||04-19-2001||03:15 AM||||Try this in an automatic carbureted car.  Drive about 70 mph, drop it into 2nd gear and nail it.  About at the redline, turn off the key, let the car coast for about 8 seconds and turn the key back on.  See what happens.  Dont do this often, unless you like rebuilding carburetors.  ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Steve Normington||04-19-2001||11:33 AM||||And people wonder why Fieros have a reputation for breaking down?!?<P>------------------<BR>85 SE 2M6||||reg||7
Z||000015||Spektyr||04-19-2001||12:15 PM||||LOL 4me, I used to do that all the time in my POS Chevy Citation.  The other way to do it is switch the ignition off, pump the gas twice and then switch it back on.<P>BOOM!<P>Takes care of those pesky tailgaters when that gigantic flame and cloud of smoke comes out the back.<P>Wreaks havoc on your muffler though...<P>hehe.  (course that car was a junker, don't try this at home if you want your car to keep running right)||||reg||1
